      "title": "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c415329d-71cf-59fb-b7d9-cd5647699874",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186179672/plots/634139bfdd72389ea0b44b217c3da9ae623667e8e2685b5793f4f1992695a00d-prompt.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $ABCA$, shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?\n\n{{selection}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, notice that we can represent $C$, traversed counterclockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC=C_1 \\cup \\{-C_2\\} \\cup \\{-C_3\\}.\n$$\n\nThe paths $C_2$, $C_3$ are traversed in the opposite direction to their arrows. Reversing a path changes the sign of its vector line integral.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r &= {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\ &= {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
